cartridge "RALLY RACER/SCOTT ELDER/1988"

' RALLY RACER
' A ColecoVision game based on "Rally Racer" by Scott Elder, COMPUTE!'s
' Gazette, November 1988 (Commodore 64, BASIC loader + machine code
' "rallycode"; also on the Best of 1988 disk with its article).
' Made for Amy Studio by Claude (an AI made by Anthropic), 2026, in Amy by
' Amy Bienvenu.
'
' The game: a top-down rally on a walled course. Each player's window
' scrolls with the car. Leave the start line, drive down to the yellow
' half-lap line at the bottom of the course, and come back over the
' checkered start/finish line. Hitting a wall stops the car dead. Two
' players race at once on a split screen (the cars never collide); one
' player races the clock. The fastest time of each track is kept until
' power-off.
'
' From the original (orig/rallycode.dis, orig/rally racer.bas, the
' article): the 80 x 40-pixel track picture, each pixel 3 x 2 characters
' of road or wall with rounded corners; Scott Elder's default track; eight
' headings, left/right turn, up shifts up, down shifts down and brakes,
' gears 0-6; a wall stops the car (back to its last position, gear 0); the
' start/finish line and the half-lap line at track pixel x 44 on rows 2-3
' and 36-37, which the editor never lets you fill; one lap: half-lap line
' first, then the finish; the split screen with blue and white cars; the
' first car home wins ("WINNER" over its half); the day's fastest time;
' the track editor (stick moves a cursor over the track picture, the
' button toggles road / wall).
'
' Measured in jsc64 (orig/c64time-rally.mjs): the C64 loop runs about 17
' times a second; in top gear the car moves a character (8 pixels) a loop
' (2.3 pixels a frame here), a held stick shifts a gear a loop and turns an
' eighth every two loops. The race runs 30 passes a second, 15 with two players
' (top gear there 2 pixels a frame, a little under the C64's 2.3).
'
' Liberties: no disk, so no Load / Save track: four built-in tracks (the
' original's default "CLASSIC" and three new ones drawn under the editor's
' rules) and one edited "CUSTOM" track kept in RAM until power-off; the
' editor shows 56 x 40 pixels at a time and scrolls, the left button
' toggles, the right one ends (the C64's f1 fill / f3 clear are left out).
' Speed grows evenly with the gear (the C64's gear 3-5 were alike); a
' diagonal is as fast as a straight. The car glides pixel by pixel; the
' window scrolls a cell at a time and looks ahead of the car. The windows
' are 13 x 18 cells (one player: 12 x 18). The clock shows real minutes,
' seconds and tenths (the C64's counted loops), with a 5:00 limit; a
' 3-2-1-GO start; one player alone races the clock; the title shows a map
' of the chosen track (a first-time player could not know the course
' otherwise). Hold a button two seconds to give up a race. The lines are
' crossed anywhere in their corridor (the C64 checked 3 of 4 cells).
'
' Controls: joysticks only. Stick left/right turn, up shifts up, down
' shifts down (gear 0 brakes). Title: up/down choose, left/right change
' the track, a button starts. Editor: stick moves, left button toggles,
' right button done.
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29, rows 1-22; tiles from code 128; the
' frame loop is protected from the NMI; screens drawn with the NMI off,
' display off. Music first in the sound table; the title tune plays once.

